overall_score: 7
magic_system: 7 -- The telepathy system is genuinely well-designed with protein-dependency creating real narrative tension. The crisp economy is charming but underutilized — it's mentioned as the pigeon currency but only appears in one scene (Chapter 6). Could a writer write a contract negotiation scene? Probably not yet. The system is testable in confrontation but not in negotiation or legal scenarios.
world_history: 6 -- The dual-layered town concept is strong and the canal-to-vault pipeline is clever. But the history feels like set dressing rather than a engine for conflict. The 'eccentric mayor' is decorative — what did he do that still matters?
geography_and_culture: 7 -- The sensory domain split (smell for Barnaby, visual for Bea) is excellent and would make locations feel different in narration. The Hard Path concept is genuinely clever. But the world needs more cultural specificity to reach 8+.
lore_interconnection: 7 -- The geography-to-plot pipeline (canal → vents → vault access) is the strongest interconnection. The crisp economy is charmingly isolated — it works but doesn't deeply affect other systems. A writer could swap out the currency without collapsing the plot, which suggests modularity.
iceberg_depth: 6 -- The iceberg is present but shallow. The planning docs do a good job of tracking what's planted and paid off, but several elements feel like 'the answer will be revealed' rather than 'the answer is X and here's how it works.' The Accountant gap is the most glaring — a writer would hit Chapter 12 and have no idea who to write.
character_depth: 8 -- Bea is the strongest character in the registry by far. Her arc from 'perfect observer' to 'chaos-embracing investigator' is clear and the chapter-by-chapter arc supports it. Henry is functional but his wound/want/need is the weakest — 'wants routine, needs to prove bravery' is a bit stock.
character_distinctiveness: 8 -- Removing tags, I could identify all speakers correctly from the exemplars. The anti-exemplars are well-chosen and define boundaries clearly. Henry's voice is the least developed but his military-jargon-plus-joint-pain combo is distinctive enough for a supporting character.
character_secrets: 7 -- The secrets that exist are well-designed. Simba's vault code theft is particularly good because it's planted (mentioned in the outline) and pays off (Chapter 19 revelation). Barnaby's pigeon negotiations are a nice character-deepening secret. But the Maya gap and the Accountant omission are significant.
outline_completeness: 8 -- This is a genuinely complete outline. A skilled author could draft from it. The chapter-by-chapter emotional arcs are present and the pacing looks right for a middle-grade mystery. The Save-the-Cat beats are approximately in the right places (opening image Ch 1, catalyst Ch 3, midpoint Ch 12, all-is-lost Ch 18, finale Ch 22).
foreshadowing_balance: 7 -- The 4 tracked threads are well-managed and the payoff chapters make sense. But the ledger covers maybe half of the significant plot devices. A writer would need to retrofit foreshadowing for the Stilton, Accountant, laundry chute, and Vivaldi Alarm during drafting.
internal_consistency: 6 -- The submersible continuity error is the most damaging — it spans three chapters and a writer would hit a dead end at Chapter 11. The timeline/outline ordering mismatch is also significant. These are not minor quibbles; they would force a writer to stop and restructure.
voice_clarity: 8 -- This is one of the stronger voice documents I've seen. The anti-exemplars are genuinely useful and the character-specific tones are well-defined. The Barnaby exemplar is particularly strong — 'without an immediate advance of cheddar' is exactly the right register. The document is undermined slightly by the structural rule inclusion and the borderline-too-adult Bea exemplar.
canon_coverage: 6 -- The Canon is functional but incomplete. A writer introducing a new fact in Chapter 5 (say, a new tunnel or a new character ability) would have no way to verify it against the canon because the canon doesn't consolidate all established facts. The three-day timeline is useful but the chapter-to-day mapping has inconsistencies with the outline ordering.
slop_in_planning_docs: N/A -- The slop is present but mild. The exemplars are good enough to be useful but a writer should be warned not to use them as templates for every line. The Barnaby and Simba patterns are the most concerning because they could infect all dialogue if followed too closely. Henry's exemplar is incomplete relative to his voice definition.
contradictions_found: ["SUBMERSIBLE CONTINUITY: Chapter 10 states the submersible 'drifts away accidentally' but Chapter 11 has the characters disarming a tripwire and reaching the Bistro basement in the sub. No recovery scene is described.", "SALAMI CONTINUITY: Chapter 10 finds the Salami in the submersible, the sub drifts away, but Chapter 13 involves 'extracting the Salami using yoga mats and Henry.' No scene explains how the Salami was recovered between these chapters.", 'TIMELINE vs OUTLINE ORDER: The Canon timeline places the boathouse discovery on Day 2 afternoon, but the outline has Chapter 14 (science fair, Day 2 morning) before Chapter 15 (boathouse). If the science fair is morning and the boathouse is afternoon, the outline order is correct but the timeline description is vague. If the boathouse is meant to come first, the outline is wrong.', "CHAPTER 16 ACTION: Barnaby 'uses his intellectual snobbery to buy time' against Simba's Heavy-Lifters. The Canon states telepathy only works on Bea and cannot compel action. Is Barnaby speaking telepathically to Bea (coaching her) or physically confronting the cats? The mechanism is unclear.", 'ACCOUNTANT CHARACTER: Appears in Chapters 12 and 20 of the outline but has no entry in the Character Registry, no species, no motivation, and no defined role. A writer would not know who this character is.', "LULU CHARACTER: Mentioned in Chapter 3 ('Lulu's balloon pop distraction') but never defined in any document. Who is Lulu? Human? Animal? Why does she have a balloon?"]
lore_score: 7
weakest_dimension: internal_consistency — the submersible and Salami continuity errors would force a writer to stop drafting and restructure mid-outline. These are not minor gaps; they are plot-breaking contradictions that span multiple chapters.
top_3_improvements: ['Fix the submersible/Salami continuity across Chapters 10-13. This is the highest-leverage fix because it resolves the most damaging contradiction and would raise internal_consistency from 6 to 8+, which would significantly impact the overall score.', "Add the Accountant to the Character Registry with full wound/want/need chain, species, and motivation. This closes the biggest character gap and would raise both character_secrets and iceberg_depth by eliminating a 'mystery wearing an iceberg costume.'", "Expand the foreshadowing ledger to cover the Sovereign Stilton, the Vivaldi Alarm, the laundry chute, and the Accountant's introduction. This is the fastest way to raise the foreshadowing_balance score and would make the outline draftable without retrofitting."]

